"comparison": "Both C and BAC address the accounting treatment for foreign currency translation and remeasurement, specifically distinguishing between functional and non-functional currency scenarios. C states that for transactions in non-functional currencies, exchange rate effects are included in 'Principal transactions', while for entities in highly inflationary economies using the U.S. dollar as their functional currency, effects are in 'Other revenue' (c_2024_8_P022_S001, c_2024_8_P022_S003). Similarly, BAC explains that when a foreign entity's functional currency is the U.S. dollar, remeasurement gains or losses are included directly in earnings (bac_2024_8_P108_S004), and when it is the local currency, translation gains/losses go to Accumulated OCI (bac_2024_8_P108_S003, bac_2024_8_P108_S002). Both disclosures align on the principle that functional currency determination drives whether gains/losses hit earnings (remeasurement) or equity (translation).",
